---
import Legal from '../layouts/Legal.astro';
import { REPO } from '../lib/releases.js';

const CONTACT = 'qrate@dvnl.work';
---

<Legal
  title="Terms of service"
  eyebrow="Terms"
  updated="15 August 2026"
  description="Terms for qrate and this website: AGPL-3.0 free software, no warranty, alpha builds, your catalogue stays yours, and the optional Google services."
  lede="qrate is free software under AGPL-3.0. These terms cover this website and the optional services the application can connect to."
>
  <h2>The software</h2>
  <p>
    qrate is licensed under the <a href="/license">GNU Affero General Public License v3.0</a>.
    That licence, not this page, governs your right to run, study, modify and share the software. If
    the two ever disagree, the licence wins. Running qrate to catalogue a collection creates no
    obligation to anyone, at any scale.
  </p>

  <h2>No warranty</h2>
  <p>
    qrate is provided <strong>as is</strong>, without warranty of any kind, express or implied,
    including merchantability and fitness for a particular purpose. To the extent the law allows, no
    contributor is liable for any damages arising from its use — including loss of data. This is the
    plain-language version of sections 15 and 16 of the AGPL, which are the operative text.
  </p>

  <h2>Alpha software</h2>
  <p>
    Current builds are alpha. They can contain defects that damage or lose a project file.
    <strong>Keep backups of anything you care about</strong>, and treat qrate as one tool in your
    workflow rather than the only copy of your collection.
  </p>

  <h2>Your data is yours</h2>
  <p>
    A project is a file on your own disk. We have no access to it, claim no ownership or licence over
    your catalogue or your linked files, and operate no service that stores them. You are responsible
    for having the right to catalogue the material you put into it, and for meeting any rules your
    own institution places on that material.
  </p>

  <h2>Connected services</h2>
  <p>
    qrate can connect to services you choose: Google Drive and Sheets for export, and whatever a
    plugin you install points at. Those connections are between you and that provider, under their
    terms — for Google, the <a href="https://policies.google.com/terms" rel="noopener"
      >Google Terms of Service</a
    >. You are responsible for having permission to use the account you connect. We do not sit in the
    middle of those connections and cannot restore data lost inside them. See the <a
      href="/privacy">privacy policy</a
    > for exactly what is sent and when.
  </p>

  <h2>Acceptable use</h2>
  <p>
    Do not use qrate or its connected integrations to break the law, to infringe someone else's
    rights, or in a way that violates a connected provider's terms. Do not represent a modified build
    as an official one; the AGPL requires modified versions to carry prominent notice of the change.
  </p>

  <h2>This website</h2>
  <p>
    The site is provided as is, and may be offline or out of date at any time. Release information is
    pulled from GitHub when the site is built, so it can lag the repository. Downloads themselves are
    served by GitHub under their terms. Builds are unsigned, so your operating system may warn on
    first launch — verify a download against the published checksums if that matters to you.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes</h2>
  <p>
    These terms may change. The date at the top of this page changes with them, and earlier versions
    stay in the site's <a href={`https://github.com/${REPO}`} rel="noopener">git history</a>.
    Continuing to use qrate after a change means you accept the current terms.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>
    Questions, or commercial licensing: <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT}`}>{CONTACT}</a>. Bugs are best
    filed as <a href={`https://github.com/${REPO}/issues`} rel="noopener">issues</a>, with steps that
    reproduce them.
  </p>
</Legal>
